The checks of tests/network: each is run by the system, as root, in an
empty directory, once it has a user guest with the password Gu3st-pw and
a user ftp, and must write what follows its "--", to the byte
(tests/harness/checks.py).  The first names the machine, brings its
Ethernet interface up and has inetd start the three daemons that
/etc/inetd.conf has commented out; the rest rely on it.

== setup
hostname pc286
echo "10.0.2.15 pc286" >> /etc/hosts
ifconfig ne0 inet 10.0.2.15 netmask 255.255.255.0 up
route add default 10.0.2.2 1 >/dev/null
cat >> /etc/inetd.conf <<END
exec stream tcp nowait root /usr/libexec/rexecd rexecd
tftp dgram udp wait nobody /usr/libexec/tcpd tftpd
ntalk dgram udp wait root /usr/libexec/ntalkd ntalkd
END
kill -1 `ps ax | grep "[i]netd" | awk '{print $1}'`
sleep 2
echo "localhost root" > /home/guest/.rhosts
chown guest /home/guest/.rhosts
hostname
ifconfig ne0 | grep -c "inet 10.0.2.15"
--
pc286
1
